<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Eyebrows</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18–27</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Red Eyebrows were a peasant rebel movement that rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> around 18 CE against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a>, its fighters painting their eyebrows red to tell friend from foe. In 25 they enthroned a Han descendant, Liu Penzi, and took and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. Starving and exhausted, they surrendered in 27 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Emperor Guangwu</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-eyebrows.jpg" alt="Red Eyebrows" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: drs2biz · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Han_soldiers_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Red Eyebrows were one of the two major peasant rebel movements against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>'s Xin dynasty, the other being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulin">Lülin</a>. Wang's land reform and a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> flood in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a> left many people unable to live by farming, and about 17 CE they rebelled in numerous, scattered risings. Fan Chong began his own in 18 CE in Ju and Langya counties, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> as a base and gathering about 10,000 men.</p><p>Mother Lü, a landowner whose son an official had executed, sold her property to recruit poor young men and stormed a county seat in 17 CE. After her death most of her followers joined Fan Chong. The rebels' only laws were that a murderer would die and a person who wounded another must care for the victim, and their leaders took modest titles such as county educator and sheriff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 22 CE the rebels, who had killed the Xin general Jing Shang, painted their eyebrows red so as to tell friend from foe when facing over 100,000 regular troops under Wang Kuang and Lian Dan. They defeated them at Chengchang, where Lian died. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lulin">Lülin</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 23 CE and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-mang">Wang Mang</a>, the Red Eyebrows leaders briefly submitted to the Gengshi Emperor, but received no real lands and went home.</p><p>In late 24 CE they marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> with two armies that joined at Hongnong in spring 25 and numbered about 300,000. They chose by lot a 15-year-old Han descendant, Liu Penzi, as emperor and took Chang'an in the autumn, and Xie Lu strangled the captive Gengshi Emperor. Their soldiers robbed the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzhong">Guanzhong</a>, who turned against them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 26 CE the Red Eyebrows burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>'s palaces and pillaged the city, then suffered famine and a cold spell that froze many soldiers to death. In spring 27 they won a victory at Hu against Deng Yu and Feng Yi by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> that left a decoy of food, but a month later Feng defeated them, colouring his own troops' eyebrows red to confuse them.</p><p>At Yiyang they met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-guangwu-of-han">Liu Xiu</a>'s overwhelming force and surrendered after he agreed to spare Liu Penzi. Their generals were settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> with stipends but no posts. Fan Chong and Pang An were executed when a plot to rebel again was found out, and Liu Xiu gave the blinded Liu Penzi farmland to live from.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Red Eyebrows” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their distress fed risings such as the <b>Red Eyebrows</b> (about 18 CE) and the Yellow Turbans (184).</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li><li><blockquote>Peasant risings such as the <b>Red Eyebrows</b> and the Lülin spread.</blockquote><a href="/state/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
